<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="glutaminyl-tRNAglⁿ biosynthesis via transamidation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5921" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The production of |FRAME: Charged-GLN-tRNAs &quot;L-glutaminyl-[tRNA&lt;SUP&gt;gln&lt;/SUP&gt;]&quot;| can occur in two different ways. In Gram-negative bacteria and in the cytosol of eukaryotic cells, there are separate tRNA synthetases for charging |FRAME: GLN-tRNAs| with |FRAME: GLN| and |FRAME: GLT-tRNAs| with |FRAME: GLT| (see |FRAME: TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|). However, Gram-positive bacteria (such as |FRAME: TAX-1423|), archaea and the chloroplasts and mitochondria of eukaryotic cells lack a synthetase for charging |FRAME: GLN-tRNAs| with |FRAME: GLN| (although in some protists and fungi it is imported from the cytosol into mitochondria). This results in a misacylation of |FRAME: GLN-tRNAs| with |FRAME: GLT| by a nondiscriminating |FRAME: EC-6.1.1.24|. A second enzyme, |FRAME: EC-6.3.5.7|, is used to transamidate the misacylated |FRAME: L-glutamyl-tRNAGln &quot;L-glutamyl-[tRNA&lt;sup&gt;Gln&lt;/sup&gt;]&quot;| to |FRAME: Charged-GLN-tRNAs|. A glutaminase subunit (cf. |FRAME: EC-3.5.1.2|) produces an ammonia molecule that is transferred by a 30 &amp;Aring; tunnel to a synthase subunit, where it is ligated to the carboxy group that has been activated by phosphorylation. In |CITS: [9342321]| and in |CITS: [18441100]|). Other tRNA-dependent amino acid transformations have been reported for asparaginyl-tRNA, selenocysteinyl-tRNA and formylmethionyl-tRNA (reviewed in |CITS: [9048478]|).</Description>
  <Authors>
